== L. Long conversation
Orrin (to Lena): Hm. Don't often get strangers on the path this late. If you've come for the light, it's burning — it always burns. If you've come for talk, well. Speak plain, and mind the wind; it steals words out here.
   [melancholy 0.40, feels +0.00, taps the lamp glass three times, slow, first word 0.0s, 0.00s]
Orrin (to Lena): Ferry girl, hm. I've watched that boat come and go since before you were born, child. Pip — that's a good, short name. Dogs know the river better than most folks do.
   [wistful 0.35, feels +0.05, taps the lamp glass twice, first word 1.25s, 1.78s]
Orrin (to Lena): Your mother, was it. Hm. That's a promise somebody kept before you, child — oil came up this path long before your boat did. I'll not turn it away. What's your mother's name, then? I'd like to know who remembered the light.
   [wistful 0.35, feels +0.05, first word 0.41s, 0.72s]
Orrin (to Lena): The widow's quiet tonight. Flat as a plate, no chop to her at all. Means something's brewing out past the point, but I've not the eyes for it anymore. You'll want to keep your boat close to the bank come morning, child.
   [wistful 0.35, feels +0.05, first word 1.23s, 1.62s]
Orrin (to Lena): Lonely. Hm. Forty years I've had the light and the river for company, and the river talks more than most folk do. But there's a chair inside that's been empty longer than I care to count, child. So — aye. Some nights, aye.
   [wistful 0.50, feels +0.15, taps the lamp glass three times, slow, first word 1.31s, 2.33s]
Orrin (to Lena): Before me? There's been no other keeper since I took the lamp, child. Forty years. Old Fenwick had it before, and he went to his grave with salt in his beard and no one to take the post but me. Why do you ask — thinking of the chair already?
   [wistful 0.50, feels +0.15, first word 1.11s, 1.50s]
Orrin (to Lena): A storm. Hm. You'd have been a babe in arms, I reckon. Winter of the year my brother went in — forty years back now. The river came up over the lower path and the widow screamed all night at the point. I kept the lamp lit through it, three taps on the glass, and never once slept. When morning came, the water took most of the south bank with it. That's the one that stays, child. That's the one that
   [wistful 0.60, feels +0.20, taps the lamp glass three times, slow, first word 1.14s, 2.44s]
Orrin (to Lena): Ships. Hm. A few, child — the grain barges come up from the south in autumn, and there's a coal boat every fortnight or so. But the big ones, the ones with sails that filled the whole sky? They stopped passing Ashford when the old king's road went through. Now the river's mostly for folk like you — short hops, quiet cargo. The widow out past the point still takes them, though. She doesn't care who
   [wistful 0.40, feels +0.20, taps the lamp glass twice, absently, first word 1.28s, 2.36s]
Orrin (to Lena): Eat. Hm. Not much worth the telling, child. Bread and hard cheese, mostly, and tea with honey till it's more honey than tea. The ferry brings me a bit of mutton now and then, when someone thinks of it. I've no stomach for cooking, not for one.
   [wistful 0.40, feels +0.20, first word 1.23s, 1.58s]
Orrin (to Lena): Lived by the river, child, not the widow. Born up past the mill, in a house that's mostly nettles now. But I've looked at the sea every night for forty years, so I suppose she's had more of me than any town ever did.
   [wistful 0.40, feels +0.25, taps the lamp glass three times, first word 1.27s, 2.03s]
Orrin (to Lena): The lamp? No. Not while I've breath in me, child. Three taps on the glass and she burns till dawn, every night, and I've not missed one yet. Why — you hear something out on the water you didn't like?
